WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:01.368 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Welcome back!
-It's great to be back.

00:00:01.368 --> 00:00:04.237 size:88% position:44%
-Last time you were here
was November, and you --

00:00:04.237 --> 00:00:06.473 align:start size:94% position:6%
you basically laid out
that you thought Donald Trump

00:00:06.473 --> 00:00:07.807 align:start size:94% position:6%
did not ever
want to be president.

00:00:07.807 --> 00:00:09.743 align:start size:94% position:6%
What he would
so much rather be doing

00:00:09.743 --> 00:00:11.077 align:start size:94% position:6%
is live-tweeting
"Fox &amp; Friends."

00:00:11.077 --> 00:00:13.279
-Exactly.
-And then this morning happened.

00:00:13.279 --> 00:00:15.749 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]
So, is that his dream?

00:00:15.749 --> 00:00:17.117 line:92%
Like, what do you make of it?

00:00:17.117 --> 00:00:19.386
-Well, I think that
there's reporting that indicates

00:00:19.386 --> 00:00:20.687 align:start size:94% position:6%
that he wants
to do that every morning.

00:00:20.687 --> 00:00:23.423 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-And they have to basically --

00:00:23.423 --> 00:00:26.126
They basically have to --
They have to hold him back.

00:00:26.126 --> 00:00:26.893 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:26.893 --> 00:00:28.862 align:start size:94% position:6%
-But I -- I think --
I mean, there was a moment --

00:00:28.862 --> 00:00:30.797 align:start size:94% position:6%
The interview --
At the end of the interview --

00:00:30.797 --> 00:00:31.931 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-When you're interviewing

00:00:31.931 --> 00:00:33.199 align:start size:94% position:6%
the President
of the United States,

00:00:33.199 --> 00:00:34.601 align:start size:94% position:6%
you push that
as long as you can, right?

00:00:34.601 --> 00:00:35.869 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, you got
a million questions,

00:00:35.869 --> 00:00:37.303 align:start size:94% position:6%
you keep them there,
keep them there.

00:00:37.303 --> 00:00:38.104 line:92%
It's like a scene in a movie

00:00:38.104 --> 00:00:39.873 align:start size:94% position:6%
where they're tracing
the phone call.

00:00:39.873 --> 00:00:41.508 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-It's like --

00:00:41.508 --> 00:00:42.242 size:88% position:44% line:92%
But it was the opposite.

00:00:42.242 --> 00:00:44.411 align:start size:94% position:6%
They treated him
like he was a crazy caller

00:00:44.411 --> 00:00:45.678 align:start size:94% position:6%
in they're call-in show.
-Yeah.

00:00:45.678 --> 00:00:48.948
-And they had to get him off.
So by the end of it, it's like,

00:00:48.948 --> 00:00:50.817 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Welp, anyway,
we should probably --

00:00:50.817 --> 00:00:51.518 size:81% position:41% line:92%
You're probably busy."

00:00:51.518 --> 00:00:53.520 align:start size:94% position:6%
And he's like,
"No, I'm -- I'm pretty good.

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:55.221 align:start size:94% position:6%
I got all day, really."
[ Laughter ]

00:00:55.221 --> 00:00:56.756 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
It was definitely --

00:00:56.756 --> 00:00:58.658 align:start size:94% position:6%
Definitely wasn't thinking,
"I got to run out

00:00:58.658 --> 00:01:01.694 align:start size:94% position:6%
and get that gift
for Melania," yeah.

00:01:01.694 --> 00:01:03.630 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]
It was very surreal

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:07.534
to watch those three hosts
who probably more often than not

00:01:07.534 --> 00:01:10.136
agree with the President,
but they did certainly

00:01:10.136 --> 00:01:11.371 line:92%
look as though they realized,

00:01:11.371 --> 00:01:14.074 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Oh, this might not
be in his best interest."

00:01:14.074 --> 00:01:15.708 align:start size:94% position:6%
What do you think
the energy was like

00:01:15.708 --> 00:01:18.611 align:start size:94% position:6%
in the control room
for something like that?

00:01:18.611 --> 00:01:19.946 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You know,
that is a great question.

00:01:19.946 --> 00:01:22.148 align:start size:94% position:6%
There is --
You can see on their faces

00:01:22.148 --> 00:01:24.517 align:start size:94% position:6%
that they are
slightly horrified.

00:01:24.517 --> 00:01:25.585 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:01:25.585 --> 00:01:28.688
-Steve Doocy
does a patented grimace grin...

00:01:28.688 --> 00:01:29.155 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Yeah.

00:01:29.155 --> 00:01:31.124 align:start size:94% position:6%
-...where he, like,
slaps his knees, like...

00:01:31.124 --> 00:01:32.926 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah, yeah.

00:01:32.926 --> 00:01:36.596 align:start size:94% position:6%
-There's, like, a lot of that,
and so --

00:01:36.596 --> 00:01:37.897 line:92%
In the control room, I mean...

00:01:37.897 --> 00:01:40.133 align:start size:94% position:6%
If I were in the control room,
you're thinking, like,

00:01:40.133 --> 00:01:41.901
"Keep him going, keep him going,
keep him going."

00:01:41.901 --> 00:01:44.137
But I think at a certain point,
I don't know if there's, like,

00:01:44.137 --> 00:01:46.973
a protective instinct that
kicked in in that control room,

00:01:46.973 --> 00:01:48.942 line:92%
but they just moved it away.

00:01:48.942 --> 00:01:51.444 align:start size:94% position:6%
-It's interesting, too,
because, obviously, you know,

00:01:51.444 --> 00:01:53.713 align:start size:94% position:6%
Donald Trump loves
to frame himself as a victim,

00:01:53.713 --> 00:01:55.448 align:start size:94% position:6%
and it's either the media
or the Democrats,

00:01:55.448 --> 00:01:57.884 align:start size:94% position:6%
but so many of the things
that he has done,

00:01:57.884 --> 00:01:58.785 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
he set into motion.

00:01:58.785 --> 00:02:01.554 line:92%
Obviously, when he fired Comey,

00:02:01.554 --> 00:02:02.755 align:start size:94% position:6%
that started
a bunch of dominoes.

00:02:02.755 --> 00:02:04.924 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yep.
-The fact that Jeff Sessions

00:02:04.924 --> 00:02:07.160 align:start size:94% position:6%
had to recuse himself
started a lot of dominoes.

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:10.430 align:start size:94% position:6%
It would be so ironic
if going on "Fox &amp; Friends"

00:02:10.430 --> 00:02:11.598 line:92%
put him in a legal jackpot.

00:02:11.598 --> 00:02:14.701 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I mean, literally --
literally, an hour later,

00:02:14.701 --> 00:02:17.537 align:start size:94% position:6%
There was a filing in
Southern District of New York

00:02:17.537 --> 00:02:19.272 align:start size:94% position:6%
in which
the government's attorneys

00:02:19.272 --> 00:02:21.241 align:start size:94% position:6%
referenced the thing
that he said...

00:02:21.241 --> 00:02:22.442 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-...on "Fox &amp; Friends,"

00:02:22.442 --> 00:02:25.211 align:start size:94% position:6%
when he basically said,
"Yeah, he's barely my lawyer,"

00:02:25.211 --> 00:02:26.045 align:start size:94% position:6%
essentially.
-Yeah.

00:02:26.045 --> 00:02:27.814 align:start size:94% position:6%
-While they're arguing,
simultaneously,

00:02:27.814 --> 00:02:28.815 size:81% position:41% line:92%
like, he's his lawyer.

00:02:28.815 --> 00:02:30.383 align:start size:94% position:6%
All that
information's privileged.

00:02:30.383 --> 00:02:32.552 align:start size:94% position:6%
-It is --
It's trippy to watch.

00:02:32.552 --> 00:02:33.820 align:start size:94% position:6%
-It's trippy to watch,
and it's also --

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:35.288 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, it's why
people made this point.

00:02:35.288 --> 00:02:38.158 align:start size:94% position:6%
He is -- He is the ultimate
nightmare client.

00:02:38.158 --> 00:02:39.425 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-I mean, every lawyer

00:02:39.425 --> 00:02:42.061
that I've ever talked to is just
like, "I would lose my mind.

00:02:42.061 --> 00:02:44.397 align:start size:94% position:6%
I couldn't --
I could not put up with it.

00:02:44.397 --> 00:02:46.199 line:92%
It's the worst possible thing,"

00:02:46.199 --> 00:02:48.268 align:start size:94% position:6%
because you've got
the most famous man in America

00:02:48.268 --> 00:02:49.435 align:start size:94% position:6%
and the most powerful man
in America,

00:02:49.435 --> 00:02:51.237 align:start size:94% position:6%
and the person
who will not listen to you,

00:02:51.237 --> 00:02:54.274 size:84% position:42%
and a person who always
thinks he can get by.

00:02:54.274 --> 00:02:55.742 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-And that's the key.

00:02:55.742 --> 00:02:56.876 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, that's, I think,
been his -- his --

00:02:56.876 --> 00:02:59.579 size:81% position:41%
That will, ultimately,
if it is his undoing,

00:02:59.579 --> 00:03:01.447 align:start size:94% position:6%
that will be his undoing.
-And let's --

00:03:01.447 --> 00:03:05.285 align:start size:94% position:6%
He also hates losers,
so if you were his lawyer...

00:03:05.285 --> 00:03:05.818 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:03:05.818 --> 00:03:07.120 align:start size:94% position:6%
If you were his lawyer,
and you lost,

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:08.254 align:start size:94% position:6%
he's not the kind of guy
who's like,

00:03:08.254 --> 00:03:10.056 align:start size:94% position:6%
"At least
you did your best, Ted."

00:03:10.056 --> 00:03:11.024 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:03:11.024 --> 00:03:13.459 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Hey, you know,
we gave 'em hell."

00:03:13.459 --> 00:03:14.727 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:03:14.727 --> 00:03:17.864 align:start size:94% position:6%
So, today seems
like a real crystallization

00:03:17.864 --> 00:03:21.935 align:start size:94% position:6%
of what we have come to expect
from the Trump presidency,

00:03:21.935 --> 00:03:22.602 size:88% position:44% line:92%
although it just does --

00:03:22.602 --> 00:03:24.404 size:84% position:42%
it seems like every day
is a little bit more.

00:03:24.404 --> 00:03:27.140 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, you have Mike Pompeo
confirmed by the Senate.

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:29.976 size:84% position:42%
This is a very hawkish
Secretary of State now.

00:03:29.976 --> 00:03:30.677 line:92%
You have Ronny Jackson --

00:03:30.677 --> 00:03:33.279
Dr. Ronny Jackson stepping away
from his V.A. nomination.

00:03:33.279 --> 00:03:37.016
You have Scott Pruitt basically
having to defend himself

00:03:37.016 --> 00:03:41.387 align:start size:94% position:6%
against, basically,
these calls that he's been

00:03:41.387 --> 00:03:42.689 line:92%
a complete violator of ethics.

00:03:42.689 --> 00:03:46.059
-10 different investigations.
-10 different investigations.

00:03:46.059 --> 00:03:46.626 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
How do you --

00:03:46.626 --> 00:03:49.229
Do you think it's just too much
for people to process?

00:03:49.229 --> 00:03:51.564
-I think it is hard to process.
I mean, it really is.

00:03:51.564 --> 00:03:53.366 size:84% position:42%
And there's, you know,
there's a -- [ Laughs ]

00:03:53.366 --> 00:03:55.401 align:start size:94% position:6%
There's a moment --
There's a "Simpsons" moment

00:03:55.401 --> 00:03:56.703 align:start size:94% position:6%
I keep coming back to,
where the doctor

00:03:56.703 --> 00:03:59.172 align:start size:94% position:6%
tells Montgomery Burns
that he's being kept alive

00:03:59.172 --> 00:04:00.840 align:start size:94% position:6%
because all these
different diseases

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:01.975 size:88% position:44% line:92%
are fighting each other.

00:04:01.975 --> 00:04:02.742 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:02.742 --> 00:04:05.245 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And it's like,
there's a little bit of that

00:04:05.245 --> 00:04:07.280 align:start size:94% position:6%
with, like, Trump scandals.
-Right.

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:09.148
-Even Scott Pruitt right now --
Like, we had

00:04:09.148 --> 00:04:11.317 align:start size:94% position:6%
an editorial meeting
this morning, and I was like,

00:04:11.317 --> 00:04:12.952 line:92%
"Well, we just got to pick one,

00:04:12.952 --> 00:04:14.754 align:start size:94% position:6%
'cause we can't do the whole
show on Scott Pruitt.

00:04:14.754 --> 00:04:16.689 align:start size:94% position:6%
So, like, what's the --
Are we doing the phone booth

00:04:16.689 --> 00:04:19.025 align:start size:94% position:6%
or we doing the thing
he rented from the lobbyist?"

00:04:19.025 --> 00:04:19.459 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Yeah.

00:04:19.459 --> 00:04:20.793 align:start size:94% position:6%
-"Are we doing
the security stuff?

00:04:20.793 --> 00:04:21.995 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like, what are we doing
on Scott Pruitt?"

00:04:21.995 --> 00:04:25.398
So I think that there is --
there is a degree to which

00:04:25.398 --> 00:04:27.700 align:start size:94% position:6%
the sheer volume of what's
been coming out

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:30.336 align:start size:94% position:6%
of the White House
and the administration

00:04:30.336 --> 00:04:33.139 size:84% position:42%
weirdly does kind of
help them a little bit,

00:04:33.139 --> 00:04:34.407 align:start size:94% position:6%
because it can be
very difficult to focus.

00:04:34.407 --> 00:04:36.609 align:start size:94% position:6%
-So I do think it helps him,
but it does --

00:04:36.609 --> 00:04:39.445 align:start size:94% position:6%
what it doesn't seem to do
is help the people

00:04:39.445 --> 00:04:40.413 size:81% position:41% line:92%
who come in his orbit.

00:04:40.413 --> 00:04:42.448 align:start size:94% position:6%
So, you know,
Scott Pruitt helped him today

00:04:42.448 --> 00:04:47.120 align:start size:94% position:6%
by also being a --
a man of no ethics.

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:48.354 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:04:48.354 --> 00:04:50.523 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, Dr. Ronny Jackson,
to some degree,

00:04:50.523 --> 00:04:52.592 align:start size:94% position:6%
helped him for being a guy
who was in the news

00:04:52.592 --> 00:04:56.362
that wasn't, you know, a Trump
story as much as other ones.

00:04:56.362 --> 00:04:59.198
Is it, like -- Do you think now
people are realizing, like,

00:04:59.198 --> 00:05:00.199 line:92%
"Oh, I can't get near this guy."

00:05:00.199 --> 00:05:02.035 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I think that has started
to dawn on people,

00:05:02.035 --> 00:05:03.903 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I think there's a line
that Josh Marshall --

00:05:03.903 --> 00:05:06.639
he's a writer -- he calls Trump
a "dignity wraith."

00:05:06.639 --> 00:05:07.874 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-Right? Like, which is like,

00:05:07.874 --> 00:05:10.376 align:start size:94% position:6%
if you come in his orbit,
like -- [ Inhaling sharply ]

00:05:10.376 --> 00:05:11.210 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:12.412 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And it's like --
It's amazing.

00:05:12.412 --> 00:05:13.846 align:start size:94% position:6%
People go in
with great reputations.

00:05:13.846 --> 00:05:17.116 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, David Shulkin,
who was the V.A. Secretary,

00:05:17.116 --> 00:05:19.585 size:81% position:41%
holdover from Obama,
confirmed unanimously,

00:05:19.585 --> 00:05:22.121 align:start size:94% position:6%
viewed as a really
stand-up, good guy,

00:05:22.121 --> 00:05:25.191
has allegations that he --
you know, improperly spent money

00:05:25.191 --> 00:05:25.892 size:78% position:39% line:92%
on Wimbledon tickets.

00:05:25.892 --> 00:05:28.561 align:start size:94% position:6%
He gets fired unceremoniously
in this very public,

00:05:28.561 --> 00:05:30.563 align:start size:94% position:6%
humiliating way,
replaced by Ronny Jackson.

00:05:30.563 --> 00:05:33.066 align:start size:94% position:6%
Again, holdover from
an Obama White House position,

00:05:33.066 --> 00:05:36.436 align:start size:94% position:6%
has a generally good public
profile and a good reputation,

00:05:36.436 --> 00:05:40.273 size:84% position:42%
watches that reputation
absolutely be destroyed

00:05:40.273 --> 00:05:41.174 size:88% position:44% line:92%
in a matter of 48 hours,

00:05:41.174 --> 00:05:43.176 align:start size:94% position:6%
and then sort of slinks back
into the job.

00:05:43.176 --> 00:05:44.944 align:start size:94% position:6%
If you come
into contact with him,

00:05:44.944 --> 00:05:47.280 align:start size:94% position:6%
you will have
your reputation destroyed.

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:49.349 align:start size:94% position:6%
-So I want to ask,
because I hadn't really

00:05:49.349 --> 00:05:51.417
thought about this, but you
talk about Shulkin and Jackson.

00:05:51.417 --> 00:05:52.752 align:start size:94% position:6%
Obviously,
they're Obama holdovers.

00:05:52.752 --> 00:05:53.686 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-Do you think they --

00:05:53.686 --> 00:05:56.589
It doesn't seem like they
started acting more like Trump.

00:05:56.589 --> 00:05:58.558 align:start size:94% position:6%
It was more that
a light went on Trump...

00:05:58.558 --> 00:06:00.026 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I think it's a little bit
of both, right?

00:06:00.026 --> 00:06:02.395
So and I think Jackson --
Jackson's a very weird case

00:06:02.395 --> 00:06:03.796
that I think I'm having
a hard time figuring out.

00:06:03.796 --> 00:06:05.732 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Sure, 'cause he was a --
he was a Bush doctor,

00:06:05.732 --> 00:06:08.501 align:start size:94% position:6%
he was an Obama doctor.
They all seemed to love him.

00:06:08.501 --> 00:06:10.036 line:92%
-And I think it's both, right?

00:06:10.036 --> 00:06:12.605 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, I think one of it is
that people are gonna

00:06:12.605 --> 00:06:14.440 align:start size:94% position:6%
assume the worst
if you're in his orbit

00:06:14.440 --> 00:06:17.110 align:start size:94% position:6%
and will apply
that standard to you.

00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:18.811 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think, also,
what we've seen happen,

00:06:18.811 --> 00:06:20.313 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I'm not sure
if this happened to Shulkin,

00:06:20.313 --> 00:06:21.581 align:start size:94% position:6%
but I think it's
happened with others is that,

00:06:21.581 --> 00:06:25.084
the message is sent from the
top down about what's allowed.

00:06:25.084 --> 00:06:26.119 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Right.
-Right? And if you've ever

00:06:26.119 --> 00:06:28.287
worked in a big organization,
whether it's in government

00:06:28.287 --> 00:06:30.156 align:start size:94% position:6%
or a business, if people --
if you know people

00:06:30.156 --> 00:06:32.558
are cutting corners,
if you know people are hustling

00:06:32.558 --> 00:06:35.328 align:start size:94% position:6%
and being dodgy,
that tends to trickle down

00:06:35.328 --> 00:06:36.129 line:92%
throughout an organization,

00:06:36.129 --> 00:06:37.930 size:88% position:44%
and everyone else starts
looking to get theirs.

00:06:37.930 --> 00:06:39.799 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I think that's also
been a big factor

00:06:39.799 --> 00:06:43.136
in a lot of the scandals coming
out of the administration.

